H4  sector lemma, no uniformity needed.  Quantified form:
    A > 60deg , G>0 on the arc, theta1 in (a, b-60deg), r close to 1
    => exists unit pair in W.
==============================================================================
  A =  60.0001 deg : slack over 60 deg = 0.000100 deg ; admissible r range = (max(1,0.9999984894), inf) intersect (1, 1+G)
  A =       61 deg : slack over 60 deg = 1.000000 deg ; admissible r range = (max(1,0.9851472064), inf) intersect (1, 1+G)
  A =       75 deg : slack over 60 deg = 15.000000 deg ; admissible r range = (max(1,0.8213398164), inf) intersect (1, 1+G)
  A =       90 deg : slack over 60 deg = 30.000000 deg ; admissible r range = (max(1,0.7071067815), inf) intersect (1, 1+G)
  A =      120 deg : slack over 60 deg = 60.000000 deg ; admissible r range = (max(1,0.5773502694), inf) intersect (1, 1+G)
  A =      170 deg : slack over 60 deg = 110.000000 deg ; admissible r range = (max(1,0.5019099188), inf) intersect (1, 1+G)

==============================================================================
H5  endpoint A = 60 deg (aperture exactly 120 deg): if the window is positive at the TWO
    directions used, r-1 < min(G1,G2) and sigma(r) < A hold for r close to 1. No rate
    comparison is needed. The rate comparison only appears if one first truncates the
    arc: using the compact subarc [a+eta, b-eta] of width A' = A - 2eta forces
    sigma(1+G_eta) < A - 2eta, i.e.  66.14 deg * G_eta > 2 eta.  Show the numbers.
==============================================================================
  eta= 0.01 deg: truncated width=59.980000 deg, need G_eta > 0.00030241  (= 0.017327 deg of arc-equivalent)
  eta=  0.1 deg: truncated width=59.800000 deg, need G_eta > 0.00303370  (= 0.173818 deg of arc-equivalent)
  eta=  1.0 deg: truncated width=58.000000 deg, need G_eta > 0.03133267  (= 1.795230 deg of arc-equivalent)
  eta=  5.0 deg: truncated width=50.000000 deg, need G_eta > 0.18310079  (= 10.490903 deg of arc-equivalent)
  -> with truncation the required window tends to 0 linearly: no uniform statement survives
     without extra input; without truncation (closed-arc window) nothing is needed.

==============================================================================
H6  counterexample: open W, positive window at every direction of an OPEN arc, but
    infimum 0 on a compact subarc.  W = {1<|z|<2} minus closed radial slits
    at angles theta_n -> theta_0, from radius 1+delta_n to 2, delta_n -> 0.
==============================================================================
  slit start radii delta_n: ['5.000e-01', '2.500e-01', '1.250e-01', '6.250e-02', '3.125e-02'] ... -> 0
  window G(theta_n) = delta_n > 0 for every n, G(theta_0) = 1 (no slit there),
  inf over the compact subarc containing theta_0 = 0.
  W is open (open minus closed). So 'window > 0 at every direction' does NOT imply
  'uniformly positive on compact subarcs'.       [this is what hinge (1) needs]

==============================================================================
verdict of the checks
==============================================================================
  H1 exact.  H2 exact (sigma<60deg strictly for r>1).  H3 exact.
  H4: unit pair exists from POINTWISE positivity on an arc of width > 60deg;
      uniformity of the window is never used.
  H5: at A = 60deg the closed-arc version also works; the truncated version needs a rate
      comparison 66.14deg * G_eta > 2eta that the source does not supply.
  H6: the uniform-width claim, as a consequence of pointwise positivity, is FALSE.
  => hinge (1) should be deleted; hinges (2),(3) are the whole argument.

==============================================================================
H7  sharp sector threshold tau(g)  [deg]:  numeric bisection vs closed form
==============================================================================
  g=    1e-6: tau_numeric= 59.999934  tau_closed= 59.999934  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=    1e-4: tau_numeric= 59.993385  tau_closed= 59.993385  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=    1e-3: tau_numeric= 59.933918  tau_closed= 59.933918  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=    0.01: tau_numeric= 59.346030  tau_closed= 59.346030  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=    0.05: tau_numeric= 56.873780  tau_closed= 56.873780  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=     0.1: tau_numeric= 54.071384  tau_closed= 54.071384  |diff|=3.33e-16
  g=     0.3: tau_numeric= 45.239730  tau_closed= 45.239730  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=     0.5: tau_numeric= 38.942441  tau_closed= 38.942441  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=     0.8: tau_numeric= 25.841933  tau_closed= 25.841933  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=    0.95: tau_numeric= 12.838568  tau_closed= 12.838568  |diff|=0.00e+00
  g=1.0    : tau_closed = 0.000000 deg (radial segment of length 1 fits)
  => MC120's 120 deg (arc width 60 deg) is the g -> 0+ limit, i.e. the WORST case.
     A thicker window excludes strictly larger apertures by the same argument.

==============================================================================
H8  explicit instance: A = 65 deg > 60 deg, window g = 0.05, centre x = 0
==============================================================================
  r = 1+g/2 = 1.025 ; sigma(r) = 58.392808819264324 deg ; need sigma < A = 65 deg : True
  both radii in the window (1, 1+g): True and True
  theta1 = 0 deg, theta2 = 58.392808819264324 deg, both inside (0,65): True
  z1 = (1.025, 0.0)
  z2 = (0.537195121951, 0.87295269113)
  |z1 - z2| = 1.0   (= 1 to 25 digits: True)
  both points lie in the sector {r u(t): 1<r<1.05, 0<t<65deg} in W, so W contains a unit pair;
  Lemma T then contradicts a.e. monochromaticity on W.
